<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the sandboxed macOS bundle - App Store/TestFlight
  distribution, built by merging tauri.conf.appstore.json over tauri.conf.json
  with the appstore Cargo feature turned on; see docs/releasing.md for the
  exact command. Not used by the Developer ID `.dmg` build, which is not
  sandboxed and carries no entitlements file at all.

  Unlike reviewer_iOS.entitlements, this one is not known to need copying back
  into a gitignored gen/ directory after a regen step - `cargo tauri build`
  for macOS has no xcodegen-driven init that wipes and rebuilds a project the
  way `cargo tauri ios init` does for gen/apple/. If that turns out not to
  hold once this is actually built through Xcode, treat it the same way as
  the iOS file and add the same kind of restore step here.

  App Sandbox is the whole point of this bundle - see git.rs and bookmark.rs
  for what it costs (no subprocess) and what it requires in return (a
  bookmark to survive a relaunch). The other two keys are what the app
  actually still needs once sandboxed:

  - files.user-selected.read-write: the folder-picker source (storage.rs) is
    read AND written - drafts are saved back into it, not just read from it.
  - network.client: every remote this app talks to - api.github.com, an
    S3-compatible bucket, a git remote over HTTPS through git-isomorphic's
    own transport - is an outgoing connection this process starts, never one
    it listens for.

  Nothing broader is granted. No iCloud or app-group keys: those exist only
  for icloud.rs and widget.rs, both `#[cfg(target_os = "ios")]` and unreachable
  from this build - see lib.rs and web/src/app/widget.js's inTauriIOS() gate.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
